et that someone  is a better swordsman than you remember  only one person can be the best at  something but that doesn't mean the  other billions of people have to be  miserable or even that that one person  will be happy at the top in fact a lot  of people feel empty when they achieve  their goal because they're looking in  the wrong place for fulfillment  fulfillment can't come from the outside  it has to come from within if you can't  be happy right now chances are you won't  be happy even when you accomplish your  goals  just to clarify again I don't think that  setting exterior goals like trying to be  the best samurai is bad but make sure  you are enjoying the journey and that  you can be happy giving you your best  even if you never become the best I  think there's also a reason that ton  zeros goal is not simply to be the  strongest samurai rather it is to do  everything he can to save his sister  while living up to his principles of  being a good moral person panchito's  raison d'être or reason for living is to  do the right  and tried to help others first and  foremost rather than being the best  there's even an interesting point where  net Sukho is about to be burned by the  Sun but Tanja recovers her the enemy  demon is about to escape and as go  kickstand Jiro in that direction  seemingly sacrificing herself so that he  could kill that demon now one of  country's main goals was saving mezack o  from the beginning but he accepts her  decision and goes after the demon even  if it means that go dying luckily nesco  doesn't die and becomes the only demon  that can withstand the sun but the point  is that even when ton zero supposed main  goal was threatened he didn't become  miserable and fall apart even if  netskope didn't make it he would have  went on doing the right thing he would  have done his duty and continued to save  people from demons that is the same way  a stoic would respond he wouldn't fall  apart because of something he can't  control he would focus on what he could  control and help in any way he could  another interesting thing is tons o  selfless or non self state in this state  hunter is the strongest version of  himself he can even temporarily  overpower Icaza and guess what's the  main characteristic of this overpowered  state it has no fighting spirit or  killing intent fighting spirit is  usually portrayed as a good thing the  more fighting spirit the more determined  you are to win the better your chances  right well not necessarily in this state  Thunder let's go of the desired outcome  he lets go of the desire to kill Icaza  and he is actually stronger as a result  how does this make sense well the great  archery master Hawk Enzo might be able  to help explain the power of detachment  for him obsessing over the outcome was  the enemy of mastering any art he  suggested his archery students put the  idea of hitting the target out of their  minds  he said and I quote the hits on the  target are only the outward proof and  confirmation of your purposelessness at  its highest of your egolessness your  self-abandonment or whatever you like to  call this state and quote the idea here  is that the more we desperately want  something or need it to happen the more  difficult it actually is to achieve in  the bhagavad-gita it is written that  work done for a reward is much lower  than work done in the yoga of wisdom  said thy heart upon thy work  never on its reward work not for the  reward but never cease to do thy work  and quote this quote should address the  concern that if you detach from the  outcome you won't feel motivated anymore  the idea is that you should do your work  like tangelo you should do the right  thing every day but you'll be even more  successful if you let go of the  desperate need to be successful when  Pancho clears his mind and just focus on  doing the dance of the fire-god properly  he is actually able to surpass Icaza but  he wasn't able to do this while he was  worrying about needing to kill a casa in  order to save gives life the key is to  focus on the process while letting go of  the outcome  thus in Austin's case he should  according to this philosophy enjoy the  process of doing his best rather than  obsessing over whether or not he will  actually become the wizard king so that  is the difference I've noticed between  demons there and a lot of others shown  and that seemed to glorify the idea that  you can be the best at anything if you  work hard enough I agree that hard work  will always increase your likelihood of  success but obsessing over the outcome  rather than focusing on the process will  hurt rather than help you according to  the philosophies we've discussed in this  video and personally I found that I'm  able to perform better and enjoy the  process more when I let go of the  outcome well let me know what you think  in the comments I'd love to hear from  you if you did enjoy this video make  sure to smash that like button to let me  know if you haven't be sure to subscribe  and ring that notification bell or you  won't get notified when new Demon Slayer  videos come out for more philosophy and  sauce about my concepts follow my  instagram at project uproar and the  project uproar youtube channel i'll link  both in the description a big thank you  goes out to our patrons over on patreon  who helped make videos like this one  possible i especially want to thank our  pro here to your patrons the one and  only gilgamesh nothing but a fan Jason  Wilson Kings ljus angel Cruz Steelers  poet Pablo atropos Wraith $30 beat Akagi  and Anatoly Cassatt's key DJ Nathaniel  and alpha do and are the one tier  patrons the ones who stay atop all clans  Stephen and Radha al-hola Optim Matty  back to 39 makoto-kun and your boy said  if you enjoy our work you can support  more of it by going over to patreon  common app for and becoming a patron  today for as little as  one dollar if you do so you'll get your  name featured in future vid